"**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nFor \\(n=3\\) it is **impossible** to have a configuration of three distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le 4\\) and contains exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. List the six required points:  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]  \n2. Observe that any line containing two of the three points  \n   \\[\n   P_1=(2,1),\\;P_2=(2,2),\\;P_3=(3,1)\n   \\]  \n   must be either vertical, horizontal, or have slope \\(-1\\); hence such a line is **non\u2011sunny**.  \n3. Consequently each sunny line can contain **at most one** of \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\).  \n4. With exactly two sunny lines we can cover at most two of these three points; the third must be covered by the single non\u2011sunny line.  \n5. Examine the three possible types of a non\u2011sunny line (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)). In each case the remaining four points cannot be covered by the two sunny lines because two of them share a common \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate, forcing a third non\u2011sunny line, which does not exist.  \n6. Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le 4\\}\n   =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there are three distinct lines  \n\\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) covering all points of \\(S\\) and that exactly two of them are sunny.  \nDenote the sunny lines by \\(L_{s}^{(1)},L_{s}^{(2)}\\) and the non\u2011sunny line by \\(L_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nAny line that contains two of the points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(2,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(2,2),\\qquad P_{3}=(3,1)\n\\]\n\nis non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three possible pairs give the following lines:\n\n* \\(P_{1}P_{2}\\): vertical line \\(x=2\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* \\(P_{1}P_{3}\\): horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis);\n* \\(P_{2}P_{3}\\): line through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) has slope \\(\\frac{1-2}{3-2}=-1\\), i.e. it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nAll three are prohibited for a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Consequence of Lemma\u202f1  \nEach sunny line can contain **at most one** of the set \\(\\{P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\}\\).  \nSince there are only two sunny lines, at most two of the three points \\(P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\) can be covered by sunny lines; the remaining point must lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a line is non\u2011sunny, it is of one of the three types:\n\n1. vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n2. horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n3. slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By definition a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. \u220e\n\n\n\nWe now treat the three possibilities for \\(L_{n}\\) separately and show that in each case the two sunny lines cannot cover the remaining points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n##### Case\u202f1: \\(L_{n}\\) is vertical, say \\(x=1\\).\n\nThen \\(L_{n}\\) covers the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe uncovered points are \\(\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}= \\{P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\}\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contain at most one of these three points, so the two sunny lines together can cover at most two of them, leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n---\n\n##### Case\u202f2: \\(L_{n}\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=1\\).\n\nNow \\(L_{n}\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nBoth \\((1,2)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, so a sunny line (which cannot be vertical) cannot contain them simultaneously.  \nThus each of the two sunny lines can contain at most one of these three points, again leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n---\n\n##### Case\u202f3: \\(L_{n}\\) has slope \\(-1\\), say \\(x+y=4\\).\n\nThe line \\(x+y=4\\) passes through \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\), so these three points are covered.  \nThe uncovered points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, while \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) share the same \\(y\\)-coordinate.  \nA sunny line cannot be vertical nor horizontal, therefore it cannot contain any pair among these three points.  \nConsequently the two sunny lines can cover at most two of them, leaving one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n---\n\nAll three cases lead to a contradiction. Hence our initial assumption is false.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with exactly }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
