"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete solution for the general integer\u202f\\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHowever, I have rigorously proved the following partial results:\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable.\n* For the smallest admissible size \\(n=3\\) **all** integers \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are attainable (explicit constructions are given).\n\nThese results constitute a substantial step toward the full answer.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Extremal values.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\):* For each lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) choose a distinct sunny line passing through that point; the slopes can be taken distinct (e.g. slope \\(a\\)) so the lines are all different and sunny.\n\n2. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* Three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* One sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\) (through \\((1,1)\\)) together with the non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=2\\) and \\(x=2\\).  \n   *\\(k=2\\):* Sunny lines \\(y=2x-1\\) (through \\((1,1)\\)) and \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\) (through \\((2,1)\\)), plus the non\u2011sunny line \\(y=2\\).  \n   *\\(k=3\\):* The three sunny lines  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}: y=2x-1,\\qquad\n   \\ell_{2}: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad\n   \\ell_{3}: y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\n   \\]\n   which are distinct, sunny, and together cover the three required points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\nThese constructions verify that for \\(n=3\\) every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n*Lemma 1.*  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with **no** sunny line (\\(k=0\\)) satisfying the covering condition.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\nEach line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); therefore the point lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Thus every required point is covered.  The lines are distinct, so the configuration satisfies all conditions with \\(k=0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2.*  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct **sunny** lines (\\(k=n\\)) satisfying the covering condition.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set of required points is\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) define the line\n\\[\n\\ell_{a,b}:\\; y = a\\,x + (b-a\\,a).\n\\]\nIts slope is \\(a\\).  Since \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), the line is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\neq (c,d)\\) then either the slopes differ (\\(a\\neq c\\)) or, when the slopes coincide, the intercepts differ because the points are distinct; consequently the lines are distinct.  \nEach line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) passes through its defining point \\((a,b)\\), so every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe required points are\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three horizontal lines\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad y=2,\\qquad y=3.\n\\]\nAll are non\u2011sunny and each point of \\(S_3\\) lies on the line whose \\(y\\)-coordinate equals its second component.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \nChoose the sunny line\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(passes through }(1,1)\\text{)},\n\\]\ntogether with the non\u2011sunny lines\n\\[\n\\ell_2:\\; y=2,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\; x=2.\n\\]\nVerification:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,1)&\\in\\ell_1,\\\\\n(1,2)&\\in\\ell_2,\\\\\n(2,1)&\\in\\ell_3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=2\\).*  \nTake the two sunny lines\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1)\\text{)},\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\quad\\text{(through }(2,1)\\text{)},\n\\]\nand the non\u2011sunny line\n\\[\n\\ell_3:\\; y=2.\n\\]\nVerification:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,1)&\\in\\ell_1,\\\\\n(2,1)&\\in\\ell_2,\\\\\n(1,2)&\\in\\ell_3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHence exactly two sunny lines appear.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=3\\).*  \nConsider the three distinct sunny lines\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1&:\\; y=2x-1,\\\\\n\\ell_2&:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\\\\n\\ell_3&:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll slopes (\\(2,-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12\\)) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the lines are sunny.  \nChecking the three required points:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,1)&\\in\\ell_1\\;(1=2\\cdot1-1),\\\\\n(2,1)&\\in\\ell_2\\;(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot2+\\tfrac32),\\\\\n(1,2)&\\in\\ell_3\\;(2=\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac32).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of the three sunny lines, giving \\(k=3\\).\n\nConsequently, for \\(n=3\\) all integers \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are attainable. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThese results settle the extremal cases and the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) for the smallest non\u2011trivial size \\(n=3\\).  Determining the admissible values of \\(k\\) for arbitrary \\(n\\ge4\\) remains open."
