e1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); therefore the point lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Thus every required point is covered.  The lines are distinct, so the configuration satisfies all conditions with \\(k=0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2.*  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct **sunny** lines (\\(k=n\\)) satisfying the covering condition.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set of required points is\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) define the line\n\\[\n\\ell_{a,b}:\\; y = a\\,x + (b-a\\,a).\n\\]\nIts slope is \\(a\\).  Since \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), the line is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\neq (c,d)\\) then either the slopes differ (\\(a\\neq c\\)) or, when the slopes coincide, the intercepts differ because the points are distinct; consequently the lines are distinct.  \nEach line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) passes through its defining point \\((a,b)\\), so every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n#### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe required points are\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three horizontal lines\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad y=2,\\qquad y=3.\n\\]\nAll are non‑sunny and each point of \\(S_3\\) lies on the line whose \\(y\\)-coordinate equals its second component.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \nChoose the sunny line\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(passes through }(1,1)\\text{)},\n\\]\ntogether with the non‑sunny lines\n\\[\n\\ell_2:\\; y=2,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\; x=2.\n\\]\nVerification:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,1)&\\in\\ell_1,\\\\\n(1,2)&\\in\\ell_2,\\\\\n(2,1)&\\in\\ell_3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=2\\).*  \nTake the two sunny lines\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1)\\text{)},\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\quad\\text{(through }(2,1)\\text{)},\n\\]\nand the non‑sunny line\n\\[\n\\ell_3:\\; y=2.\n\\]\nVerification:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,1)&\\in\\ell_1,\\\\\n(2,1)&\\in\\ell_2,\\\\\n(1,2)&\\in\\ell_3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHence exactly two sunny lines appear.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=3\\).*  \nConsider the three distinct sunny lines\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1&:\\; y=2x-1,\\\\\n\\ell_2&:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\\\\n\\ell_3&:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll slopes (\\(2,-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12\\)) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the lines are sunny.  \nChecking the three required points:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,1)&\\in\\ell_1\\;(1=2\\cdot1-1),\\\\\n(2,1)&\\in\\ell_2\\;(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot2+\\tfrac32),\\\\\n(1,2)&\\in\\ell_3\\;(2=\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac32).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of the three sunny lines, giving \\(k=3\\).\n\nConsequently, for \\(n=3\\) all integers \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are attainable. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThese results settle the extremal cases and the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) for the smallest non‑trivial size \\(n=3\\).  Determining the admissible values of \\(k\\) for arbitrary \\(n\\ge4\\) remains open.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule