 the first coordinate.  Each pair determines a line of slope \\(1\\), which is sunny.  The number of pairs is exactly \\(n-1\\); thus we obtain \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  All points of \\(S_n\\) lie on one of these lines, and the construction works for every \\(n\\ge4\\).  (For \\(n=3\\) the only admissible values are \\(k=0,1\\); the case \\(k=2\\) is ruled out by Lemma\u202f1.)  \n\n7. **Construction for \\(k=n-2\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  Take the two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) (non\u2011sunny).  The uncovered points are those with \\(b\\ge3\\); there are \\(m(2m+1)\\) of them.  Pair them as  \n   \\[\n   (1,3)\\leftrightarrow(2,4),\\;(1,4)\\leftrightarrow(2,5),\\dots ,\\;(1,m+2)\\leftrightarrow(2,m+3),\n   \\]\n   and continue with the next columns:\n   \\[\n   (3,3)\\leftrightarrow(4,4),\\;(3,4)\\leftrightarrow(4,5),\\dots\n   \\]\n   Every pair lies on a line of slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny).  The total number of pairs is exactly \\(n-2\\); consequently we obtain a configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n\n8. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-2\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n   Let \\(n=2m\\).  Suppose a configuration with exactly two non\u2011sunny lines exists.  Each non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; consequently it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the two non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(2n=4m\\) points.  The remaining \\(|S_n|-4m = \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2n = m(2m-1)-2m = 2m^2-3m\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-2=2m-2\\) sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can cover at most two points, so the sunny lines can cover at most \\(2(2m-2)=4m-4\\) points.  Since  \n\n   \\[\n   2m^2-3m > 4m-4\\qquad\\text{for all }m\\ge2,\n   \\]\n\n   the remaining points cannot be covered \u2013 a contradiction.  Therefore no configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) exists when \\(n\\) is even.  \n\n9. **Collecting the results.**  \n   * \\(k=0\\) is always possible.  \n   * For every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-3\\) a construction exists (Step\u202f5).  \n   * \\(k=n-1\\) is possible for all \\(n\\ge4\\) (Step\u202f6).  \n   * \\(k=n-2\\) is possible exactly when \\(n\\) is odd (Step\u202f7) and impossible when \\(n\\) is even (Step\u202f8).  \n   * \\(k=n\\) is impossible (Step\u202f3).  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-2\\;\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n   This description is valid for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\n**The final answer is therefore \\(\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-2\\text{ (when }n\\text{ is even)}\\}}\\).**"

