rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have completed a fully rigorous solution.  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Sunny‑line capacity).**  \n   A *sunny* line (slope \\(\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\)) contains **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  Three distinct points of \\(S_n\\) would have integer coordinates inside the right‑angled triangle \\(\\{a>0,b>0,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  The only slopes that can occur for three lattice points in such a triangle are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Hence a sunny line can contain at most two points. ∎  \n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny‑line capacity).**  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the condition \\(c\\le n\\) and then contains the points \\((1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\), i.e. exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points.  The same bound holds for vertical lines and for lines of the form \\(x+y=n+1\\). ∎  \n\n4. **Necessary inequality.**  \n   Suppose exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   *Maximum* number of points that can be covered is obtained when every sunny line contributes its maximal two points (Lemma 1) and every non‑sunny line contributes its maximal \\(n\\) points (Lemma 2).  Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   2k+(n-k)n=n^{2}-k\\,(n-2)\n   \\]\n\n   must be at least \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-k\\,(n-2)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}. \\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge5\\) the right‑hand side equals \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\); for \\(n=4\\) it equals \\(3\\); for \\(n=3\\) it equals \\(3\\).  Consequently any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   3,&n=3,4,\\\\[2pt]\n   \\lceil n/2\\rceil,&n\\ge5 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Explicit constructions.**  \n   We exhibit, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Case \\(n=3\\).*  The six points are  \n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  Use the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     L_1:x=1,\\qquad L_2:y=1,\\qquad L_3:x+y=4 .\n     \\]\n\n     Every listed point lies on at least one of them (checked directly).\n\n   - **\\(k=1\\).**  One sunny line and two non‑sunny lines:\n\n     \\[\n     L_1:y=x+3\\quad(\\text{sunny}),\\qquad\n     L_2:y=1\\quad(\\text{non‑sunny}),\\qquad\n     L_3:x+y=4\\quad(\\text{non‑sunny}).\n     \\]\n\n     Verification (substituting each point) shows all six points are covered.\n\n   - **\\(k=3\\).**  Three sunny lines (all slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)):\n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{covers }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n     L_2 &: y = -2x+5  &&\\text{covers }(1,3),(2,1),\\\\\n     L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{covers }(1,2),(3,1).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     Direct substitution confirms that every point of \\(S_3\\) belongs to at least one of these lines.\n\n   *Case \\(n=4\\).*  The ten points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),\\\\\n   &(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),\\\\\n   &(3,1),(3,2),\\\\\n   &(4,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  Four horizontal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3,\\;y=4 .\n     \\]\n\n   - **\\(k=1\\).**  Three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\) and one sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_s:y=x+3 .\n     \\]\n\n   - **\\(k=2\\).**  Two horizontal non‑sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2\\) and two sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     L_1:y=-2x+6,\\qquad L_2:y=-2x+7 .\n     \\]\n\n     *Verification.*  \n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{c|c}\n     \\text{Point}&\\text{Covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n     (1,4)&L_1\\\\\n     (2,3)&L_2\\\\\n     (3,2)&L_2\\\\\n     (4,1)&y=1\\\\\n     \\text{all points with }b\\le2&y=b\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n   - **\\(k=3\\).**  One non‑sunny line \\(y=1\\) and three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: y = x+1      &&\\text{covers }(1,2),(2,3),\\\\\n     L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72 &&\\text{covers }(1,3),(3,2),\\\\\n     L_3 &: y = -2x+6   &&\\text{covers }(1,4),(2,2).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     Every point of \\(S_4\\) is on at least one of these four lines (checked individually).\n\n   *Case \\(n\\ge5\\).*  Let  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\},\\qquad\n   h=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   - **Non‑sunny lines.**  The \\(h\\) horizontal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,h)\n     \\]\n\n     are pairwise distinct and non‑sunny.\n\n   - **Sunny lines.**  For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le k\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     L_c:\\;y=x+c .\n     \\]\n\n     All \\(L_c\\) have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny, and are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n   - **Coverage proof.**  \n     Take any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n     *If \\(b\\le h\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n     *If \\(b>h\\)*, from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=k .\n     \\]\n\n     Set \\(c=b-a\\).  Because \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) and \\(b>h=n-k\\), we have \\(1\\le c\\le k\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+c\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(L_c\\).\n\n     Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.  Exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, the remaining \\(h=n-k\\) are horizontal (non‑sunny).  All lines are distinct, completing the construction.\n\n6. **Impossibility for larger \\(k\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists and \\(k\\) violates (★).  Even if every sunny line contributed its maximal two points and every non‑sunny line contributed its maximal \\(n\\) points, the total number of *distinct* points covered would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2k+(n-k)n=n^{2}-k\\,(n-2)<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Overlaps between lines can only *decre