3. **Sufficiency constructions.**  \n   We now exhibit, for every integer \\(k\\) obeying (1), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).  The constructions are verified point‑by‑point.\n\n   *Case \\(n=3\\).*  The six points of \\(S_3\\) are listed explicitly.  The three families of lines given below are checked directly; each family contains the required number of sunny lines.\n\n   *Case \\(n=4\\).*  The ten points of \\(S_4\\) are listed explicitly.  Four families of lines (for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\)) are written down and each point is shown to lie on at least one of the lines.\n\n   *General case \\(n\\ge5\\).*  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   h=n-2k\\qquad(\\text{note that }h\\ge0).\n   \\]\n\n   **(i) Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Use the \\(h\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,h)\n   \\]\n\n   and the \\(n- k-h = k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are non‑sunny and are pairwise distinct; together they give \\(h+k=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   **(ii) Sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\;y = t\\,x + (k+1-t).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_t\\) is the positive integer \\(t\\); therefore each \\(L_t\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.\n\n   **(iii) Coverage proof.**  \n   Take any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\le h\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n   *If \\(b>h\\) and \\(a\\le k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   *If \\(b>h\\) and \\(a>k\\)*, set  \n\n   \\[\n   a' = a-k,\\qquad b' = b-h .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a'+b' = (a-k)+(b-h) \\le n+1-(k+h)=k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover \\(a',b'\\ge1\\).  Hence \\((a',b')\\) belongs to the reduced triangle  \n\n   \\