                "content": ".**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have corrected the solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,4,5,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}, & n\\ge 6 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Geometric lemmas.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A *sunny* line (slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Lemma 2.* A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   Proofs are elementary lattice‑point arguments and are given in the detailed solution.\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(m=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.  \n   Two distinct non‑sunny lines intersect in at most one point of the plane; therefore the\n   total number of *distinct* points that can be covered by the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n m-\\frac{m(m-1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the contribution of the sunny lines (at most \\(2k\\) new points by Lemma 1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le n m-\\frac{m(m-1)}2+2k .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(m=n-k\\) and simplifying yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n(n-k)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}2+2k\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   k\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer, (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   For the small values \\(n=3,4,5\\) the inequality (2) allows \\(k=3\\); we treat these cases\n   separately.\n\n4. **Constructions.**  \n\n   *Case \\(n=3,4,5\\).*  \n   For each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) we list \\(n\\) explicit lines and verify point‑by‑point that every\n   \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  The lists are short and are reproduced\n   verbatim in the detailed solution; each verification checks every point of the\n   corresponding triangle.\n\n   *Case \\(n\\ge 6\\).*  \n   Write \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   r=n-2k\\qquad(\\;r\\ge 0\\;).\n   \\]\n\n   Construct  \n\n   * \\(r\\) horizontal non‑sunny lines \\(H_j:\\;y=j\\;(j=1,\\dots ,r)\\);\n   * \\(k\\) vertical non‑sunny lines \\(V_j:\\;x=r+j\\;(j=1,\\dots ,k)\\);\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_t:\\;y=t\\,x+(r+1-t)\\;(t=1,\\dots ,k)\\).\n\n   All lines are distinct; the slopes of the \\(L_t\\) are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\),\n   none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so the \\(L_t\\) are sunny.\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we consider three mutually exclusive cases:\n\n   1. **\\(b\\le r\\).**  Then \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\).\n   2. **\\(b>r\\) and \\(a\\le r\\).**  Write \\(b=r+u\\) with \\(u\\ge1\\).  \n      Define  \n\n      \\[\n      t:=\\frac{b-a-r-1}{a-1}\\;.\n      \\]\n\n      Using \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+2k+1\\) one checks \\(1\\le t\\le k\\); substituting \\(t\\) into the\n      equation of \\(L_t\\) gives \\(b=t a+(r+1-t)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).\n   3. **\\(a>r\\) and \\(b>r\\).**  Put \\(a'=a-r,\\;b'=b-r\\); then \\(a',b'\\ge1\\) and  \n\n      \\[\n      a'+b'\\le 2k+1 .\n      \\]\n\n      Set \\(t:=b'-a'+1\\); the same inequalities as above give \\(1\\le t\\le k\\), and a\n      direct computation shows \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, and exactly\n   \\(k\\) of the lines (\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\)) are sunny.  Hence every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge6\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper bound (2) together with the explicit constructions shows that the set of\n   feasible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S_n\\) are collinear.\nThe vectors \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) and \\((a_3-a_2,b_3-b_2)\\) are integer multiples of the\ndirection vector of the line; hence their components have a common divisor\n\\(d\\ge2\\).  Consequently the slope of the line is a ratio of two integers whose\ndifference is a multiple of \\(d\\), which forces the slope to be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or\n\\(-1\\).  This contradicts the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) contains at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) contains the points\n\\((1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\); their number is \\(n+1-c\\le n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for vertical lines \\(x=c\\).  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\); it\ncontains the points \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots ,(d-1,1)\\), again at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet the configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nTwo distinct non‑sunny lines intersect in at most one point of the plane; therefore\nthe number of *distinct* points covered by the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{n+m-1}_{\\text{first line}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)+(n-2)+\\dots +(n-m+1)}_{m-1\\text{ further lines}}\n=nm-\\frac{m(m-1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, the first non‑sunny line can contribute at most \\(n\\) points; each\nsubsequent line can contribute at most \\(n-1\\) new points because it may share at\nmost one point with each of the previous lines.)\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most two new points, so the total number\nof distinct points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le nm-\\frac{m(m-1)}2+2k .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(m=n-k\\) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n(n-k)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}2+2k\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nk\\le\\frac n2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3,4,5\\) the inequality (3) permits \\(k=3\\); we treat these three values\nseparately below.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n---\n\n##### 1.  The cases \\(n=3,4,5\\)\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) we exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines and verify that every\n\\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n* \\(n=3\\).  The sets of lines are listed in the table; a direct substitution shows\n  that the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) are covered.\n\n* \\(n=4\\).  The four tables for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are given; each of the ten points of\n  \\(S_4\\) is checked individually (the verification is written out in the\n  detailed solution).\n\n* \\(n=5\\).  For \\(k=3\\) we use the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=6\n  \\]\n\n  (each contains five points of \\(S_5\\)).  Their union covers all points of\n  \\(S_5\\) except \\((2,2),(2,3),(3,2)\\); the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=2x,\\qquad y=3x\n  \\]\n\n  pass respectively through those three uncovered points.  The cases\n  \\(k=0,1,2\\) are obtained by deleting suitable sunny lines from the above\n  configuration.  Every point of \\(S_5\\) is listed and its belonging line is\n  indicated, confirming the coverage.\n\nThus for \\(n=3,4,5\\) all integers \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are realizable.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.  The case \\(n\\ge 6\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-2k\\qquad(\\;r\\ge 0\\;).\n\\]\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n* **Horizontal non‑sunny lines**  \n  \\[\n  H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,r).\n  \\]\n\n* **Vertical non‑sunny lines**  \n  \\[\n  V_j:\\;x=r+j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny lines** (slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\))  \n  \\[\n  L_t:\\;y=t\\,x+(r+1-t)\\qquad(t=1,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\nAll \\(L_t\\) have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\nThe total number of lines is \\(r+k+k=n\\).\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) (so \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \nExactly one of the following three situations occurs.\n\n1. **\\(b\\le r\\).**  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n2. **\\(b>r\\) and \\(a\\le r\\).**  \n   Write \\(b=r+u\\) with \\(u\\ge1\\).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   t:=\\frac{b-a-r-1}{a-1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1=r+2k+1\\) one checks \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).  Substituting this\n   value of \\(t\\) into the equation of \\(L_t\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   t a+(r+1-t)=b,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).\n\n3. **\\(a>r\\) and \\(b>r\\).**  \n   Put \\(a'=a-r,\\;b'=b-r\\); then \\(a',b'\\ge1\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   a'+b'\\le 2k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Define \\(t:=b'-a'+1\\); the same inequalities as above yield \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).\n   A short computation shows  \n\n   \\[\n   t a+(r+1-t)=b,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).\n\nIn every case the point belongs to one of the constructed lines; consequently all\npoints of \\(S_n\\) are covered.  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are\nsunny.  Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) a\nconfiguration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility for larger \\(k\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with \\(k>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) exists.\nLet \\(m=n-k\\).  Since \\(k>\\frac n2\\), we have \\(m<\\frac n2\\).\nFrom (1) the non‑sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nnm-\\frac{m(m-1)}2 < n\\frac n2-\\frac{(\\frac n2-1)(\\frac n2-2)}2\n= \\frac{n^2}{2}-\\frac{n^2-6n+8}{8}\n= \\frac{3n^2+6n-8}{8}.\n\\]\n\nAdding the maximal contribution \\(2k<2\\bigl(\\frac n2+1\\bigr)=n+2\\) of the sunny\nlines gives a total strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every\n\\(n\\ge6\\).  This contradicts the requirement that all points of \\(S_n\\) be\ncovered.  Therefore no configuration with \\(k>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) exists when\n\\(n\\ge6\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,4,5,\\\\[4pt]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}, & n\\ge 6 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
