all Q points: Q1 assigned to L3, Q2 not assigned yet, Q3 assigned to L2. Q2 = (2,2) is not covered. We need to cover Q2. It can be covered by any line that passes through (2,2). Which lines can we adjust? L1 passes through (1,4) and (2,1). Does it also pass through (2,2)? No. L2 passes through (2,3) and (3,1). Does it pass through (2,2)? No. L3 passes through (3,2) and (1,3). Does it pass through (2,2)? Let's check L3 equation: slope -0.5, passes through (3,2): y-2 = -0.5(x-3) => y = -0.5x + 3.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5, not 2. So no. L4 passes through (4,1) and (1,2): slope -1/3, equation: y-1 = -1/3 (x-4) => y = -1/3 x + 4/3 + 1 = -1/3 x + 7/3. At x=2, y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not 2. So none cover (2,2). So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nWe could assign Q2 to L1 by pairing P1 with Q2 instead of R2. But we need to keep R2 covered. Let's try a different assignment: maybe we can assign Q2 to L2. Pair P2=(2,3) with Q2=(2,2) gives vertical line x=2, not sunny. So not allowed. Pair P3=(3,2) with Q2=(2,2) gives horizontal line y=2, not sunny. Pair P4=(4,1) with Q2=(2,2) gives slope = (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, sunny. So we could assign Q2 to L4. But L4 currently passes through (4,1) and (1,2). If we change L4 to pass through (4,1) and (2,2) instead, slope = (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, sunny. Then we need to cover (1,2) (R1) elsewhere. R1 could be covered by L2 maybe? Pair P2=(2,3) with R1=(1,2) slope = (2-3)/(1-2) = (-1)/(-1) = 1, sunny. So we could set L2 to pass through (2,3) and (1,2) instead of Q3. Then we need to cover Q3=(3,1) elsewhere. Q3 could be covered by L3 maybe? Pair P3=(3,2) with Q3=(3,1) vertical line x=3, not sunny. Pair P4=(4,1) with Q3=(3,1) horizontal line y=1, not sunny. Pair P1=(1,4) with Q3=(3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. So we could assign Q3 to L1. L1 currently passes through (1,4) and (2,1). If we change L1 to pass through (1,4) and (3,1), slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. Then we need to cover R2=(2,1) elsewhere. R2 could be covered by L3 maybe? Pair P3=(3,2) with R2=(2,1) slope = (1-2)/(2-3) = (-1)/(-1) = 1, sunny. So L3 could be (3,2) and (2,1). L3 currently passes through (3,2) and (1,3). We can change L3 to (3,2) and (2,1). That line slope = 1, sunny. Then we need to cover Q1=(1,3) somewhere. Q1 could be covered by L2 maybe? L2 currently passes through (2,3) and (1,2). Does it also pass through (1,3)? No. Pair P2=(2,3) with Q1=(1,3) gives horizontal line y=3, not sunny. Pair P4=(4,1) with Q1=(1,3) slope = (3-1)/(1-4) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, sunny. But L4 currently passes through (4,1) and (2,2). Could change L4 to (4,1) and (1,3) slope = (3-1)/(1-4) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, sunny. Then we need to cover Q2=(2,2) which we previously assigned to L4, but now L4 changed. Q2 could be covered by L3 maybe? L3 currently passes through (3,2) and (2,1). Does it also pass through (2,2)? No. Pair P3=(3,2) with Q2=(2,2) gives horizontal line y=2, not sunny. Pair P1=(1,4) with Q2=(2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. L1 currently passes through (1,4) and (3,1). Could change L1 to (1,4) and (2,2) slope = -2, sunny. Then we need to cover Q3=(3,1) which was previously covered by L1. Now L1 changed, so Q3 not covered. Q3 could be covered by L2 maybe? L2 currently passes through (2,3) and (1,2). Pair P2=(2,3) with Q3=(3,1) slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. So L2 could be (2,3) and (3,1). Then we need to cover R1=(1,2) which was previously covered by L2. Now L2 changed, so R1 not covered. R1 could be covered by L4 maybe? L4 currently passes through (4,1) and (1,3). Pair P4=(4,1) with R1=(1,2) slope = (2-1)/(1-4) = 1/(-3) = -1/3, sunny. So L4 could be (4,1) and (1,2). Then we need to cover Q1=(1,3) which was previously covered by L4. Now L4 changed, so Q1 not covered. Q1 could be covered by L3 maybe? L3 currently passes through (3,2) and (2,1). Pair P3=(3,2) with Q1=(1,3) slope = (3-2)/(1-3) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, sunny. So L3 could be (3,2) and (1,3). Then we need to cover R2=(2,1) which was previously covered by L3. Now L3 changed, but L3 still passes through (2,1)? No, L3 now passes through (3,2) and (1,3). So R2 not covered. R2 could be covered by L1 maybe? L1 currently passes through (1,4) and (2,2). Pair P1=(1,4) with R2=(2,1) slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, sunny. So L1 could be (1,4) and (2,1). But L1 currently passes through (1,4) and (2,2). So we need to adjust again. This is a complex assignment problem. But it seems plausible that a solution exists.\n\nGiven the difficulty, we suspect that the answer might be correct: all k from 0 to n-1 always possible, and k=n possible only for odd n. But we need to verify if k=n is impossible for even n. Let's try to prove impossibility for even n.\n\nSuppose n is even, and we have n sunny lines covering all points. Since each line is sunny, it is not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or slope -1. So each line intersects each diagonal D_t at at most one point. In particular, each line intersects D_{n+1} at at most one point. Since D_{n+1} has n points, we need each line to intersect D_{n+1} at exactly one point (to cover all n points). So each line passes through a distinct point of D_{n+1}. So we have a bijection between lines and points of D_{n+1}.\n\nNow consider D_{n} (t=n). D_n has n-1 points. Since each line can intersect D_n at at most one point, we need to assign each of the n-1 points to distinct lines. So there will be one line that does not intersect D_n at any point (i.e., does not cover any point of D_n). That's okay.\n\nNow consider D_{n-1} (t=n-1). D_{n-1} has n-2 points. Each line can intersect D_{n-1} at most one point. So we need to assign each of those points to distinct lines. So we need at least n-2 lines covering them, which we have.\n\nThus, from a combinatorial perspective, it's possible.\n\nBut there might be a parity argument: For even n, the sum of slopes or something might cause a contradiction. Let's think about the slopes of lines that intersect D_{n+1} at points (i, n+1-i). The line through (i, n+1-i) and (j, n+1-j) would have slope = (n+1-j - (n+1-i)) / (j - i) = (i - j) / (j - i) = -1. So any line connecting two points on D_{n+1} has slope -1, which is not sunny. So each line can intersect D_{n+1} at only one point, as we said. So each line is determined by a point on D_{n+1} and some other point not on D_{n+1}.\n\nNow consider the parity of the sum of x-coordinates of intersection points with D_{n+1}. The points on D_{n+1} are (i, n+1-i) for i=1,...,n. The x-coordinate i runs from 1 to n. So the sum of x-coordinates is n(n+1)/2. If we consider the lines, maybe there is a parity condition on the slopes or something that prevents covering all points for even n.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the solution's answer is correct and the construction for k=n for odd n is valid, but for even n it's impossible. Let's try to find a proof of impossibility for even n.\n\nSuppose we have n sunny lines covering all points. Consider the set of points on D_{n+1} (n points). Each line passes through exactly one of them. So each line is determined by that point and some other point(s). Now consider the set of points on D_{n} (n-1 points). Since each line can intersect D_n at most one point, we need to assign each of the n-1 points to distinct lines. So there will be exactly one line that does not intersect D_n. Let's denote that line as L0. L0 passes through some point (i0, n+1-i0) on D_{n+1} and does not intersect D_n. Since D_n is the line x+y=n, which is parallel to D_{n+1} (both slope -1). So L0 is not parallel to D_n (since it's sunny). So L0 intersects D_n at some point (maybe not integer). But we need integer points of D_n to be covered by other lines.\n\nNow consider D_{n-1} (n-2 points). Each line can intersect D_{n-1} at most one point. So we need to assign each of those points to distinct lines. Since we have n lines, we can assign them. So far no contradiction.\n\nThus, combinatorial constraints do not forbid k=n for even n. So maybe the answer is indeed k=n for all n, not just odd n. But the solution says only odd n. Let's test n=4 again more thoroughly: can we find a construction with 4 sunny lines covering all points? Let's try a systematic approach: we need to assign each point (a,b) with a+b ≤ 5 to a line. Let's denote lines L_i for i=1..4. Each line must be sunny (slope not 0, ∞, -1). Each line must be distinct. We can assign each line to a distinct point on D_5: (1,4), (2,3), (3,2), (4,1). Then each line can also pass through other points. Let's try to find a solution using a simple pattern: For each i from 1 to n, define line L_i passing through (i, n+1-i) and (i, 1) maybe? That would be vertical line x=i, not sunny. So not allowed. How about line through (i, n+1-i) and (i+1, 1)? For i=1: (1,4) and (2,1) slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, sunny. For i=2: (2,3) and (3,1) slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. For i=3: (3,2) and (4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, not sunny (slope -1). So not allowed. For i=4: (4,1) and (5,1) but (5,1) not in S (since a+b ≤5, (5,1) has sum 6 >5). So not allowed.\n\nAlternatively, line through (i, n+1-i) and (1, i) maybe? For i=1: (1,4) and (1,1) vertical, not sunny. So not good.\n\nMaybe we can use lines that intersect D_5 and D_4, D_3, D_2 in a systematic way. For each i, we can pair (i, n+1-i) with (i, n-i) maybe? That would be vertical line x=i, not sunny. So not good.\n\nMaybe we can use lines that intersect D_5 and D_4 at points that are not aligned vertically/horizontally. For each i, we can pair (i, n+1-i) with (i+1, n-i) (point on D_{n+1} shifted). Let's test: For i=1: (1,4) and (2,3) are both on D_5? Actually (2,3) is also on D_5. So line connecting them has slope -1, not sunny. So not good.\n\nMaybe we can pair (i, n+1-i) with (i+1, n-i-1) which is on D_{n-1}? Let's compute: D_{n-1} is x+y = n-1 = 3 for n=4. Points on D_3: (1,2), (2,1). So not many. So maybe we need to pair each D_5 point with a point on D_4 or D_3 or D_2.\n\nLet's try to assign each line to a point on D_5 and a point on D_4 (if possible). D_4 points: (1,3), (2,2), (3,1). There are 3 points, but we have 4 lines, so one line will not have a D_4 point. Let's try to assign:\n\n- L1: (1,4) with (2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny.\n- L2: (2,3) with (3,1) slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, sunny.\n- L3: (3,2) with (1,3) slope = (3-2)/(1-3) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, sunny.\n- L4: (4,1) with no D_4 point.\n\nNow we need to cover D_3 points: (1,2), (2,1). We can assign them to lines that have not yet intersected D_3. Let's see which lines intersect D_3 currently:\n\n- L1: passes through (1,4) and (2,2). Does it intersect D_3? Solve line equation: slope -2, passes through (1,4): y = -2(x-1)+4 = -2x+6. Intersection with D_3: x+y=3 => y = 3 - x. Set -2x+6 = 3 - x => -2x + x = 3 - 6 => -x = -3 => x = 3, y = 0. So intersection at (3,0) not in S. So L1 does not intersect D_3 at integer points.\n\n- L2: passes through (2,3) and (3,1). Equation: slope -2, passes through (2,3): y = -2(x-2)+3 = -2x+7. Intersection with D_3: -2x+7 = 3 - x => -2x + x = 3 - 7 => -x = -4 => x = 4, y = -1, not in S. So L2 does not intersect D_3.\n\n- L3: passes through (3,2) and (1,3). Equation: slope -0.5, passes through (3,2): y-2 = -0.5(x-3) => y = -0.5x + 3.5. Intersection with D_3: -0.5x + 3.5 = 3 - x => bring terms: -0.5x + x = 3 - 3.5 => 0.5x = -0.5 => x = -1, y = 4, not in S. So L3 does not intersect D_3.\n\n- L4: currently only passes through (4,1). We can define L4 to pass through (4,1) and one of the D_3 points. Let's try (4,1) with (1,2): slope = (2-1)/(1-4) = 1/(-3) = -1/3, sunny. So L4: (4,1) and (1,2). That covers (1,2). Now we need to cover (2,1). We need a line that passes through (2,1) and some Pi (maybe already used). Could we assign (2,1) to L1? L1 passes through (1,4) and (2,2). Does it also pass through (2,1)? No. L2 passes through (2,3) and (3,1). Does it pass through (2,1)? No. L3 passes through (3,2) and (1,3). Does it pass through (2,1)? Let's check: line L3 equation: y = -0.5x + 3.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5, not 1. So no. L4 currently passes through (4,1) and (1,2). Does it also pass through (2,1)? Let's check: slope -1/3, equation: y-1 = -1/3 (x-4) => y = -1/3 x + 4/3 + 1 = -1/3 x + 7/3. At x=2, y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not 1. So no.\n\nThus (2,1) is not covered. We could change assignments: maybe assign (2,1) to L2 by pairing (2,3) with (2,1) vertical line x=2, not sunny. So not allowed. Pair (3,2) with (2,1) slope = (1-2)/(2-3) = (-1)/(-1) = 1, sunny. So we could change L3 to pass through (3,2) and (2,1) instead of (1,3). Then we need to cover (1,3) elsewhere. (1,3) could be covered by L1 maybe? L1 passes through (1,4) and (2,2). Does it also pass through (1,3)? No. L2 passes through (2,3) and (3,1). Does it also pass through (1,3)? No. L4 passes through (4,1) and (1,2). Does it also pass through (1,3)? No. So we need to adjust again.\n\nAlternatively, we could assign (2,1) to L1 by pairing (1,4) with (2,1) slope = -3, sunny. Then L1 would be (1,4) and (2,1). Then we need to cover (2,2) which was previously covered by L1. (2,2) could be covered by L2 maybe? L2 currently (2,3) and (3,1). Does it also pass through (2,2)? No. L3 currently (3,2) and (1,3). Does it also pass through (2,2)? No. L4 currently (4,1) and (1,2). Does it also pass through (2,2)? No. So we need to adjust again.\n\nThus constructing a solution for n=4 is nontrivial but seems plausible. However, we need to verify if it's possible at all. Let's try a systematic approach: we need to assign each point (a,b) to a line. Since we have 4 lines, each line can cover at most 4 points (since there are 4 diagonals). Actually each line can intersect each diagonal at most one point, so each line can cover at most 4 points (one per diagonal). Since there are 4 diagonals (t=2,3,4,5), each line can cover at most 4 points. So total coverage capacity is at most 4*4 = 16 points, but we only need to cover 10 points (n(n+1)/2 = 4*5/2 = 10). So capacity is enough.\n\nNow we need to assign each point to a line such that each line gets at most one point per diagonal. This is a bipartite matching problem: we have lines (L1..L4) each associated with a distinct point on D_5 (the \"anchor\" point). For each diagonal D_t (t=2..5), we need to assign each point on D_t to a line such that the line's anchor point is not on the same diagonal (except for D_5). For D_5, each line is anchored at a distinct point. For D_4, we need to assign each of its 3 points to distinct lines (since each line can cover at most one point on D_4). So we need a matching between the 3 points of D_4 and 4 lines, leaving one line unused for D_4. Similarly for D_3 (2 points) and D_2 (1 point). So we need to find a matching such that for each assignment (anchor point Pi, point Q on D_t), the line through Pi and Q is sunny (i.e., slope not 0, ∞, -1). So we need to avoid vertical/horizontal lines and slope -1.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: given the set of anchor points Pi = (i, n+1-i) for i=1..n, and the set of points Q on each diagonal D_t (t=2..n+1), can we assign each Q to a distinct Pi (for each diagonal) such that the line PiQ is not horizontal, vertical, or slope -1? For each diagonal D_t, we need to assign each point Q ∈ D_t to a distinct Pi (maybe the same Pi can be used for multiple diagonals, but each Pi can be used at most once per diagonal). So we need to find a system of distinct representatives for each diagonal.\n\nThis is reminiscent of a Latin square or a rook placement problem. For n even, maybe there is a parity obstruction: for each Pi, the line PiQ has slope (b - (n+1-i)) / (a - i). For Q = (a,b) with a+b = t, we have b = t - a. So slope = (t - a - (n+1-i)) / (a - i) = (t - n - 1 - a + i) / (a - i) = ((t - n - 1) + (i - a)) / (a - i) = ((t - n - 1) - (a - i)) / (a - i) = (t - n - 1) / (a - i) - 1.\n\nThus slope = (t - n - 1) / (a - i) - 1. For t = n+1 (the anchor diagonal), t - n - 1 = 0, so slope = -1 (as expected). For t < n+1, t - n - 1 is negative. So slope = negative number divided by (a - i) minus 1