7). That's 6 points covered. We need to cover remaining 9 points with 3 lines, each covering at most 3 points. Let's see if we can find three more lines covering 3 points each. The remaining points after covering those 6 are: Let's list all points and mark those covered.\n\nAll points:\n\n(1,1) covered by y=x.\n(2,2) covered by y=x.\n(3,3) covered by y=x.\n\n(1,5) covered by -2x+7? Actually (1,5) is on y = -2*1 + 7 = 5, yes. So (1,5) covered.\n(2,3) covered by -2x+7? y = -2*2+7 = 3, yes. So (2,3) covered.\n(3,1) covered by -2x+7? y = -2*3+7 = 1, yes. So (3,1) covered.\n\nNow remaining points:\n\n(1,2)\n(1,3)\n(1,4)\n(2,1)\n(2,4)\n(3,2)\n(4,1)\n(4,2)\n(5,1)\n\nThat's 9 points.\n\nWe need 3 sunny lines to cover these 9 points, each covering 3 points. Let's see if we can find lines covering 3 points among these.\n\nPotential lines:\n\n- Slope 1 line y = x + 1: passes through (1,2),(2,3) (already covered), (3,4) not allowed. So only (1,2). Not enough.\n\n- Slope 1 line y = x + 2: passes through (1,3),(2,4),(3,5) not allowed. So (1,3),(2,4) are both in remaining set. That's 2 points.\n\n- Slope 1 line y = x + 3: passes through (1,4),(2,5) not allowed. So (1,4) only.\n\n- Slope -2 line y = -2x + 6: passes through (1,4),(2,2),(3,0). (2,2) already covered, (1,4) remaining, (2,2) covered. So only (1,4) new.\n\n- Slope -2 line y = -2x + 5: passes through (1,4),(2,3) (covered), (3,2) (remaining). So (1,4) and (3,2) new.\n\n- Slope -2 line y = -2x + 4: passes through (1,3),(2,2) (covered), (3,1) (covered). So (1,3) new.\n\n- Slope -2 line y = -2x + 3: passes through (1,2),(2,1) both remaining. So (1,2),(2,1) new.\n\n- Slope -2 line y = -2x + 2: passes through (1,1) covered, (2,0) not allowed.\n\n- Slope -1/2 line y = -0.5x + b: For b = something to get integer points. Let's find lines that pass through (4,1) and (2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -0.5. So line L: y = -0.5x + b. Plug (2,2): 2 = -1 + b => b = 3. So line y = -0.5x + 3 passes through (2,2) (covered), (4,1) (remaining). Also passes through (6,0) not allowed. So covers (4,1) new.\n\n- Another line: (5,1) and (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-5) = 1/(-2) = -0.5. So line y = -0.5x + b. Plug (3,2): 2 = -1.5 + b => b = 3.5. So line y = -0.5x + 3.5 passes through (3,2) (remaining), (5,1) (remaining). Also passes through (1,3) (remaining). Indeed, for x=1, y = -0.5 + 3.5 = 3, so (1,3) is also on this line. So line L2: y = -0.5x + 3.5 covers (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). That's 3 points! Good.\n\nNow we have covered (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). Good.\n\nRemaining points after covering those: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). Also (1,5) already covered, (2,3) covered, (3,1) covered, (1,5) covered, (2,3) covered, (3,1) covered. So left: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). That's 6 points.\n\nWe have used lines: y=x (covers 3), y=-2x+7 (covers 3), y=-0.5x+3.5 (covers 3). That's 3 lines used. We need 2 more lines to cover remaining 6 points. Let's try to find lines covering 3 points each.\n\nPotential line: slope -2 line y = -2x + 3 passes through (1,2),(2,1) (both remaining). Also passes through (3,0) not allowed. So only 2 points.\n\nSlope -2 line y = -2x + 6 passes through (1,5) (covered), (2,4) (remaining), (3,3) (covered). So (2,4) new.\n\nSlope -2 line y = -2x + 5 passes through (1,4),(2,3) (covered), (3,2) (covered). So (1,4) new.\n\nSlope -2 line y = -2x + 4 passes through (1,3) (covered), (2,2) (covered), (3,1) (covered). So no new.\n\nSlope -2 line y = -2x + 2 passes through (1,1) (covered), (2,0) not allowed.\n\nThus we can cover (1,4) and (2,4) using two different lines: y = -2x + 5 (covers (1,4)), y = -2x + 6 (covers (2,4)). But each line only covers one new point (plus some already covered). So not efficient.\n\nMaybe we can use slope 1 line y = x + 2 covering (1,3) (covered), (2,4) (remaining). So that covers (2,4). Already covered (1,3) but that's okay. So line L4: y = x + 2 covers (2,4). Also (3,5) not allowed. So only (2,4). Not enough.\n\nSlope 1 line y = x + 3 covers (1,4) only.\n\nSlope 1 line y = x - 1 covers (2,1),(3,2) (covered), (4,3) not allowed. So line L5: y = x - 1 covers (2,1). Also (1,0) not allowed. So only (2,1).\n\nSlope -0.5 line y = -0.5x + 3 passes through (2,2) (covered), (4,1) (remaining). So line L6: y = -0.5x + 3 covers (4,1). Also (6,0) not allowed. So only (4,1).\n\nSlope -0.5 line y = -0.5x + 2.5 passes through (1,2) (remaining), (3,1) (covered). So line L7: y = -0.5x + 2.5 covers (1,2). Also (5,0) not allowed.\n\nSlope -0.5 line y = -0.5x + 4.5 passes through (1,4) (remaining), (3,3) (covered), (5,2) not allowed. So line L8: y = -0.5x + 4.5 covers (1,4). Also (3,3) covered.\n\nSlope -0.5 line y = -0.5x + 3.5 we already used.\n\nSlope -0.5 line y = -0.5x + 5.5 passes through (1,5) (covered), (3,4) not allowed, (5,3) not allowed.\n\nSlope 2 line y = 2x - 1 passes through (1,1) (covered), (2,3) (covered). So no new.\n\nSlope -3 line y = -3x + 7 passes through (1,4) (remaining), (2,1) (remaining). So line L9: y = -3x + 7 covers (1,4) and (2,1). That's 2 new points! Good.\n\nNow remaining points after covering (1,4) and (2,1) are (4,2) and (4,1) maybe? Actually we still have (4,2) and (4,1) left. Also maybe (2,4) left? Let's track.\n\nWe have used lines:\n\n1. y = x (covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3))\n2. y = -2x + 7 (covers (1,5),(2,3),(3,1))\n3. y = -0.5x + 3.5 (covers (1,3),(3,2),(5,1))\n4. y = -3x + 7 (covers (1,4),(2,1))\n\nNow covered points: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,5),(2,3),(3,1),(1,3),(3,2),(5,1),(1,4),(2,1). That's 11 points. Remaining points: (1,2),(1,5) already covered, (2,4),(4,1),(4,2). Also (2,5) not in set. Let's list all points again and mark covered:\n\nAll points:\n\n(1,1) covered\n(1,2) not covered\n(1,3) covered\n(1,4) covered\n(1,5) covered\n\n(2,1) covered\n(2,2) covered\n(2,3) covered\n(2,4) not covered\n\n(3,1) covered\n(3,2) covered\n(3,3) covered\n\n(4,1) not covered\n(4,2) not covered\n\n(5,1) covered\n\nSo remaining: (1,2),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). That's 4 points. We have used 4 lines, need 1 more line (since total lines must be 5). Can we find a sunny line covering all 4 remaining points? Let's see if any line passes through all four. Unlikely. Let's see if any line passes through three of them, and the fourth is already covered? But we have no more lines left. So this configuration fails.\n\nThus we need a different selection of lines. But maybe there is a known solution for odd n that uses all sunny lines. The solution claims that for odd n, k=n is possible. They propose using L_t for t=2,...,n+1 (i.e., all lines L_t). But we have shown that L_t does not contain D_t. So their construction is flawed. However, maybe there is a different construction that works for odd n, but they didn't provide it. The solution's reasoning is invalid because they claim L_t contains D_t, which is false. So the reasoning is invalid. Therefore Level 2 fails.\n\nBut we need to check Level 1: Is the answer correct? The answer they gave is: if n even, k ∈ {0,...,n-1}; if n odd, k ∈ {0,...,n}. Is this answer correct? We need to verify if this answer is indeed correct. Let's try to see if there is any known solution for this problem. The problem is from some contest. Let's try to reason about the possible k.\n\nWe need to find all possible numbers of sunny lines among n lines covering the triangular lattice points. The answer they gave seems plausible: for even n, you cannot have all n lines sunny; for odd n, you can have all n sunny. But we need to verify if this is indeed correct. Let's try to see if we can find a construction for odd n with all sunny lines. For n=3, we found a construction with 3 sunny lines. For n=5, we attempted but didn't find a construction. Let's try to find a systematic construction for odd n.\n\nMaybe there is a known solution: For odd n, you can use lines of slopes 1, -2, -1/2, etc. There might be a pattern: For n odd, you can partition the points into n lines each with slope either 1 or -2 or -1/2, etc. Let's try to find a pattern for n=5. Perhaps we can use lines of slopes 1, -2, -1/2, 2, -3? Let's try to find a set of 5 lines that cover all 15 points. We need each line to cover exactly 3 points (since 5*3=15). So we need a partition of the points into 5 lines each covering 3 points. Let's try to find such a partition.\n\nWe already have two lines covering 3 points each: y = x (covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3)). And y = -2x + 7 (covers (1,5),(2,3),(3,1)). That's 6 points. We need to cover remaining 9 points with 3 lines each covering 3 points. The remaining points are:\n\n(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),\n(2,1),(2,4),\n(3,2),\n(4,1),(4,2),\n(5,1).\n\nWe need to partition these into 3 lines of 3 points each. Let's try to find lines that cover 3 points among these.\n\nPotential line: y = -0.5x + 3.5 covers (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). That's 3 points! Good. So we can use that line. That covers (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). Now remaining points: (1,2),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(4,1),(4,2). That's 6 points.\n\nNow we need two lines covering these 6 points, each covering 3 points. Let's try to find a line covering (1,2),(2,1),(3,0) not allowed. So line through (1,2) and (2,1) has slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So cannot use that line. So we need a different line.\n\nConsider line through (1,2) and (4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, allowed (since not -1,0,∞). Equation: y - 2 = -1/3 (x-1) => y = -1/3 x + 2 + 1/3 = -1/3 x + 7/3. For integer points, we need x such that y integer. For x=1, y=2 (ok). For x=4, y = -4/3 + 7/3 = 3/3 = 1 (ok). For x=7, y = -7/3 + 7/3 = 0 (not allowed). So line L10: y = -1/3 x + 7/3 passes through (1,2) and (4,1). Does it also pass through (2,? )? For x=2, y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3 not integer. So only those two integer points. So line covers only 2 points.\n\nConsider line through (1,4) and (4,2): slope (2-4)/(4-1) = -2/3, allowed. Equation: y - 4 = -2/3 (x-1) => y = -2/3 x + 2/3 + 4 = -2/3 x + 14/3. For x=1, y=4 (ok). For x=4, y = -8/3 + 14/3 = 6/3 = 2 (ok). For x=7, y = -14/3 + 14/3 = 0 (not allowed). So line L11 passes through (1,4) and (4,2). Only those two integer p