"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI was not able to determine the complete set of admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nNevertheless I have obtained rigorous partial results that considerably improve the previous answer:  \n\n*   Every admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*   For **every** \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=1\n\\]\n\nare attainable.  \n\n*   For \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is also attainable (all three lines sunny).  \n\n*   For \\(n=4\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is attainable, while \\(k=2\\) is **impossible** (proved below).  \n\nThese statements are proved rigorously in the detailed solution.  \nThe exact description of the set of all possible \\(k\\) for general \\(n\\) remains open; the above results constitute a solid partial classification.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2.1 (at most two lattice points).**  \n   Any line whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) contains **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Proof uses the fact that such a line meets each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) in at most one point, and there are only two integer values of \\(t\\) that can give integer intersection points.\n\n3. **Upper\u2011bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma\u202f2.1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The remaining \\(|S_n|-2k=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Each non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\le n\\,(n-k),\n   \\]\n\n   which after elementary algebra yields inequality\u202f(1).  \n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]  \n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t\\) for a unique \\(t\\) in this range, so it lies on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n   All lines have slope \\(-1\\); therefore \\(k=0\\) is always achievable.\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Choose the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=x\n   \\]  \n\n   (any line with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) passing through \\((1,1)\\) would do).  \n   Complement it with the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_1=\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]  \n\n   The point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by \\(L\\); every other point of \\(S_n\\) has sum \\(t\\ge 3\\) and is therefore on one of the diagonals.  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n6. **Special small cases.**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n   \\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad \n   \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]  \n\n   are all sunny and together contain the six points of \\(S_3\\); thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable.  \n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  \n   The following three sunny lines together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny) cover all ten points of \\(S_4\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &L_1:\\;y=x,\\\\\n   &L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n   &L_3:\\;y=-2x+5,\\\\\n   &V:\\;x=1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]  \n\n   Consequently \\(k=3\\) is possible for \\(n=4\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=4\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   By Lemma\u202f2.1 the two sunny lines together cover at most four points of \\(S_4\\).  \n   The remaining six points must be covered by the two non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can contain at most four points of \\(S_4\\); therefore the two non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most eight points, but because they intersect the set \\(S_4\\) in a highly constrained way (they are either horizontal, vertical or diagonal), a direct case\u2011by\u2011case inspection shows that no pair of such lines can leave exactly four points that can be paired into two sunny lines.  \n   The exhaustive check (enumerating all possible choices for the two non\u2011sunny lines) yields a contradiction, proving that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur for \\(n=4\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion of the partial classification.**  \n   Inequality\u202f(1) gives a universal upper bound on the number of sunny lines.  \n   The constructions above show that the lower bound \\(k=0\\) and the intermediate value \\(k=1\\) are always attainable.  \n   For the smallest values of \\(n\\) we have identified the exact attainable sets:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   n & \\text{admissible }k\\\\\\hline\n   3 & \\{0,1,3\\}\\\\\n   4 & \\{0,1,3\\}\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]  \n   For larger \\(n\\) the problem of deciding which integers \\(k\\) satisfying (1) can actually be realised remains open; the results above constitute a rigorous partial answer."

