\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=1\n\\]\n\nare attainable.  \n\n*   For \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is also attainable (all three lines sunny).  \n\n*   For \\(n=4\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is attainable, while \\(k=2\\) is **impossible** (proved below).  \n\nThese statements are proved rigorously in the detailed solution.  \nThe exact description of the set of all possible \\(k\\) for general \\(n\\) remains open; the above results constitute a solid partial classification.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 2.1 (at most two lattice points).**  \n   Any line whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) contains **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Proof uses the fact that such a line meets each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) in at most one point, and there are only two integer values of \\(t\\) that can give integer intersection points.\n\n3. **Upper‑bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 2.1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The remaining \\(|S_n|-2k=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   Each non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\le n\\,(n-k),\n   \\]\n\n   which after elementary algebra yields inequality (1).  \n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]  \n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t\\) for a unique \\(t\\) in this range, so it lies on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n   All lines have slope \\(-1\\); therefore \\(k=0\\) is always achievable.\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=