ence for every \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}.}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Explicit constructions (verification point\u2011by\u2011point).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\) (no sunny line).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\) of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n   Every line in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is of the forbidden form \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\), i.e. non\u2011sunny.  \n   Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line).**  \n   Take the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;y=x\n   \\]\n\n   (slope \\(1\\), not parallel to any forbidden direction).  \n   Complement it with the diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_1=\\{\\,x+y=t\\mid t=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Verification for each point of \\(S_n\\):\n\n   * If \\((a,b)=(1,1)\\) (sum\u202f2) then \\(b=a\\), so the point lies on \\(L_s\\).  \n   * If \\(a+b\\ge3\\) then the point lies on the unique diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_1\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered, exactly one line (\\(y=x\\)) is sunny, and the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *\\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).**  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Their incidences with the six points of \\(S_3\\) are:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{Point}&\\text{Line containing it}\\\\\\hline\n   (1,1)&\\ell_1\\;(1=1)\\\\\n   (2,2)&\\ell_1\\;(2=2)\\\\\n   (1,2)&\\ell_2\\;(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52)\\\\\n   (3,1)&\\ell_2\\;(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52)\\\\\n   (1,3)&\\ell_3\\;(3=-2\\cdot1+5)\\\\\n   (2,1)&\\ell_3\\;(1=-2\\cdot2+5)\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence all six points are covered and all three lines are sunny.\n\n7. **Excluding the remaining values.**  \n\n   *For \\(n\\ge4\\)* we have proved \\(k\\le1\\) (steps\u202f4\u20135) and exhibited constructions for \\(k=0,1\\); therefore no other \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\n   *For \\(n=3\\)* the counting inequality (2) gives \\(k\\le3\\).  \n   Constructions exist for \\(k=0,1,3\\) (step\u202f6).  \n   To rule out \\(k=2\\), note that a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) (as argued in step\u202f5).  \n   With exactly two sunny lines we could cover at most two of these three points, while the remaining non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one of them (it would have to be the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\), which already covers the two points of sum\u202f3, leaving \\((1,1)\\) uncovered).  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Conclusion** \u2013 the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
