ses the three-point obstruction to get k ≤ 1 for n≥4. The three-point obstruction is valid. However, we need to check if there is any flaw in the reasoning that would make the method invalid. The solution's Lemma 2 seems a bit complicated but appears correct. The counting argument yields N_max ≥ |S_n| leads to k(k-1) ≥ 0, which is always true, so they note that counting alone does not forbid large k. Then they use the three-point obstruction to get k ≤ 1. The three-point obstruction is valid. However, we need to verify that the three-point obstruction indeed forces k ≤ 1 for n≥4. Let's examine the logic: They claim any sunny line can contain at most one of the three points (1,1),(1,2),(2,1). That's true. So to cover these three points, we need at least three lines, and at most one of them may be sunny. So we need at least two non-sunny lines. If k≥2, then we have at most n-2 non-sunny lines. For n≥4, n-2 ≥ 2, so we have at least two non-sunny lines. So that condition is satisfied. So the three-point obstruction alone does not forbid k≥2. But they then argue that we need three distinct lines to cover the three points, but only n-2 non-sunny lines are allowed; the remaining line would have to be sunny, contradicting the fact that at most one sunny line can contain a point of the set. Wait, they say \"we need three distinct lines to cover the three points, but only n-2 non-sunny lines are allowed; the remaining line would have to be sunny, contradicting the fact that at most one sunny line can contain a point of { (1,1),(1,2),(2,1) }.\" Let's parse: For k≥2, we have n-k non-sunny lines. Since k≥2, n-k ≤ n-2. So we have at most n-2 non-sunny lines. But we need three distinct lines to cover the three points. At most one of those three lines can be sunny, so we need at least two non-sunny lines among them. That's okay because we have at most n-2 non-sunny lines, which for n≥4 is at least 2. So we can allocate those two non-sunny lines to cover two of the points, and the t