nny lines). We can choose c=6 as the third non-sunny line. Then non-sunny lines cover c=6 (5 points). So after that, points covered:\n\n- c=2: covered by non-sunny line c=2 (point (1,1)). Also y=x covers (1,1) but that's okay.\n- c=3: covered by non-sunny line c=3 (points (1,2),(2,1)).\n- c=5: covered by non-sunny line c=5 (points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)).\n- c=6: covered by non-sunny line c=6 (points (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)). y=x also covers (3,3) but that's okay.\n- c=4: points (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). (2,2) covered by y=x. (1,3) and (3,1) remain uncovered.\n\nWe have one sunny line left (the second sunny line). It can intersect c=4 at most once, covering either (1,3) or (3,1). So one point remains uncovered. So fails.\n\nTry using non-sunny lines c=2,4,5 (instead of c=2,3,5). Then we have non-sunny lines covering c=2 (1 point), c=4 (3 points), c=5 (4 points) = 8 points. Sunny lines: y=x covers (1,1) (c=2) already covered, (2,2) (c=4) already covered, (3,3) (c=6). The other sunny line can cover at most one point from each diagonal not fully covered: c=3 (2 points), c=6 (5 points). We have not used c=3 or c=6 as non-sunny lines. So we need to cover c=3 (2 points) and c=6 (5 points) with the remaining sunny line. That's impossible because one sunny line can intersect each diagonal at most once, so can cover at most 2 points (one from c=3, one from c=6). So fails.\n\nTry using non-sunny lines c=2,4,6. Then non-sunny lines cover c=2 (1), c=4 (3), c=6 (5) = 9 points. Sunny lines: y=x covers (1,1) (c=2) already covered, (2,2) (c=4) already covered, (3,3) (c=6) already covered. The other sunny line can cover at most one point from each diagonal not fully covered: c=3 (2 points), c=5 (4 points). So at most 2 points, leaving many uncovered. So fails.\n\nTry using non-sunny lines c=3,4,5. Then non-sunny lines cover c=3 (2), c=4 (3), c=5 (4) = 9 points. Sunny lines: y=x covers (1,1) (c=2), (2,2) (c=4) already covered, (3,3) (c=6). So we have covered c=2 (1 point) and c=6 (5 